so if we were to  write sequentially to each slot in  memory we first write to the first slot  on page N and then the first slot on  page n plus one and then the first slot  on page n plus 2 all the way until  eventually run out of pages and then we  loop back they go to the second slot on  page n to write our 16th slot so we've  just kind of taken our representation  before rotated and flipped it 90 degrees  when we do that you'll notice that now  the byte that were writing to that C is  in the same position as it is in our  integer representation and in the page  you'll notice the F there that gives us  the page that that particular C F  address goes to is actually in the same  position the nipples are in the exact  same place so instead of having to shift  that C over and shift that EFT over to  translate our address instead what we  can do is just do an and operation just  kind of wipe out with zeros to see on  one side that gives us one value and  then wipe out the F on the other side  that gives us the other value so again  here's our program before and here's our  program afterwards  assembly programming is doing a lot of  little things like this you right now  algorithm you get it  to work and then you figure out if you  play around with the numbers if there's  a smaller and faster way to write your  program so what's next for n Ruby I'd  like to finish up version 1.0 obviously  and I'm working very actively on that  the reason why n Ruby isn't on github  right now is because there are a couple  of little libraries in there that it's  using currently for its developing it  that are actually not open source and so  legally I don't want to get in trouble  for releasing not open source software  however in version 1.1 or 1.0 I'm going  to strip all that out so that I can put  up a github if you want you to follow me  on Twitter I'll announce it when it  finally comes out once I do have it  working I am curious to try getting and  Ruby working on other processors I'm  especially interested in the msp430  which is a really cool microprocessor  that's a more modern one especially  those you get super low-power variants  so for example you can have a  microprocessor that runs on a lemon with  a copper plate and a zinc plate put in  it like you see in science class I would  love the idea of Ruby this like heavy  weight inefficient programming language  running on the power of a lemon I think  that would be fantastic and also the  msp430 is more powerful so it's a little  bit easier to write code for it I also  want to try getting and working on other  computers that use the 6502 especially  the NES the Nintendo Entertainment  System wouldn't be go to write a game  that runs in Ruby and that Ruby is  actually running on the Nintendo  Entertainment System the thing that's  gonna make this very challenging and the  reason why I mix it in it is the nes  only had two kilobytes of RAM so that's  where all your data would go and then  your source code would be in 40  kilobytes of problem so you have a lot  of ROM space you don't have that much  space actually store the object so be  relatively interesting at least for me  to do that so thank you so much for all  of your time if you have any questions  feel free to speak  [Applause]
 

